As I posted five or six months back, the natural existing tool to
accomplish this task, and certainly equally amusing, is a tin opener.

If you want to add stuff to the game to make it more "realistic" [I'm
growing to hate that word more with every additional Keven posting...],
then a skinning knife, a punch awl, and a needle and strong cord [Cat
gut? Here, kitty, kitty.] would seem like the minimum requirement, plus
a significant amount of sit down time. This could perhaps more simply
come in the form of an "armorer's kit", and then the game could have a
whole new dimension as another poster's suggestions for what armor to
make out of what monster were implemented.

Back to the original question of polypiling, I really liked another
poster's suggestion of making a golem out of the accumulated scrap. One
could allow polypiling, while slowing its actually utilization down a
whole lot, by seeding the code with dozens of combinations of stuff,
that, if they occur in the same pile, get used up in the construction of
something large, hairy, bad tempered, nasty, and armed, if appropriate
with some of the rest of the pile, and standing right there in your
face. Double the motivation for not wanting to meet this little surprise
by making polymorph wands double ended, the other end pointed at your
possessions less the wand, with the percent chance of your possessions
being affected by "reflections off the pile" at one per item in the
pile(s), max 100, so that the only safe way to polypile is unarmed and
unarmored, and only the truly foolhardy or desperate would "zap that
zap" more than once or twice a game.

